  "metadata": {
    "id": "pw8-fx-node-tree",
    "name": "FX NODE TREE",
    "author": "Patchwork Eight Engineering",
    "description": "A short plucky square-wave hit (Filter 1 bandpass for character) demonstrating both new FX bank slot types in one patch: a subtle Chorus in the layer's first insert slot (gentle width, not the star of the show), and a 4-node NodeDelay tree in the first master slot -- informed by ChowMatrix's 'tree of delay nodes' concept (docs/FX_BANK.md). Node 0 taps the dry signal directly; nodes 1 and 2 are both children of node 0 (each delays node 0's OWN output further, not the original input); node 3 is a child of node 1, three generations deep. Each node has its own pan, feedback, and distortion, so the resulting texture is a genuinely branching multi-tap glitch, not a simple repeating echo -- exactly the kind of complex, non-repeating delay network ChowMatrix's manual describes, adapted to this codebase's fixed-capacity realtime-safety discipline (kMaxDelayNodes=6) rather than an unboundedly growable tree.",
